# SMSC destination rewrite and delivery timing: the changes to
# ~/docker_open5gs/smsc/kamailio_smsc.cfg on the voice VM (mounted into the `smsc` container).
# Fragments to place by hand, not a whole file: the rest of that file is the docker_open5gs
# project's and holds the database URL, which stays out of Git.
#
# Before editing: cp kamailio_smsc.cfg kamailio_smsc.cfg.bak-norm-2026-11-11
#                 cp kamailio_smsc.cfg kamailio_smsc.cfg.bak-timer-2026-11-12
# After editing:  docker restart smsc
# Rollback:       cp kamailio_smsc.cfg.bak-norm-2026-11-11 kamailio_smsc.cfg && docker restart smsc
# Kamailio 6.2 (the image the voice VM runs). Rule 1 applied and verified 2026-11-11, the timing
# change 2026-11-12.
#
# WARNING: restarting this container empties its message store. Any message waiting to be delivered
# at that moment is lost. Restart when nothing is in flight.

# --- 1. Destination rewrite: in route[SMS_FROM_3GPP], immediately after -------------------------
#       dp_translate("1", "$avp(to)/$avp(to)");

		# Ratel: national 0XXXXXXXXXX to 234XXXXXXXXXX
		if ($avp(to) =~ "^0[0-9]{10}$") {
			$avp(to) = "234" + $(avp(to){s.substr,1,0});
		}

# --- 2. Delivery timing: the rtimer parameter near the top of the file ---------------------------
#       was interval=30, and the comment above it said 30 seconds
# Time interval for SMS retries is set to 10 seconds
modparam("rtimer", "timer", "name=sms;interval=10;mode=1;")

# --- 3. Retry limit: in route[SMS_WORKER], the threshold and its message -------------------------
#       was `> 2` and "after 2 retries"
			if ($sht(sms_retries=>$dbr(q=>[$var(i),0])) > 6) {
				xlog("Dropping SMS [$dbr(q=>[$var(i),3])] TO $dbr(q=>[$var(i),2]) after 6 retries \n");


# ================================================================================================
# Why rule 1
# ================================================================================================
# Phones address an SMS with the number as the user typed or saved it, and the destination sits
# inside the message body, so the S-CSCF's request-URI normalisation does not reach it. The keypad
# handset sends 0XXXXXXXXXX. The SMSC looked for a phone registered under that exact string, found
# none because the stored identity is 234XXXXXXXXXX, and dropped the message after its retries:
#
#   ERROR: Dropping SMS [hi] TO 02064730451 after 2 retries
#
# Messages from the iPhone always worked because it sends 234XXXXXXXXXX already. So the symptom was
# one-directional and looked like a phone problem rather than a numbering one.
#
# Verified on 2026-11-12: a message from the keypad handset to the iPhone was logged as
# `TO 2342064730451` and delivered, and the reverse direction still worked.
#
# ================================================================================================
# Why rules 2 and 3 together
# ================================================================================================
# The SMSC does not deliver on receipt. It stores the message and a background worker sends whatever
# is waiting. On the first message to a given destination the worker only subscribes to that phone's
# registration state and sends nothing, so a first message needed two rounds of the timer. At 30
# seconds that measured 53 and 51 seconds end to end.
#
# The interval alone cannot be shortened: the retry limit counts rounds, not time, so going from 30
# to 10 seconds would have cut the window in which a sleeping phone can be paged and answer from
# about 90 seconds to about 30. Raising the limit from 2 to 6 keeps roughly the same real window
# while making delivery about three times quicker.
#
# Measured after the change on 2026-11-12: 16 and 18 seconds for a first message to each phone. A
# later message to the same phone should arrive inside one round.
#
# Watch for duplicates. If a phone takes longer than 10 seconds to acknowledge, the worker can send
# the same message again on the next round and the user would see it twice. Not observed on this
# network, where both phones acknowledged in well under a second.
